{:fh/id  "FH-CTRL-009"
 :fh/law "The reset target is the caller's CURRENT baseline, re-read by the render that follows the reset rather than restored from a mount-time snapshot; and reaching it costs no render-time mutation — repeating the render any number of times, including under candidates the host never selects and across a hot-reload body-generation advance, dispatches nothing, moves no controller state, and yields an identical tree."

 :kind :my.ui/buffered-field

 :control [:invoice 42 :amount]

 ;; Mount-time facts. If the reset restored a snapshot taken here, the
 ;; assertions below would show this value rather than the caller's
 ;; current one.
 :baseline "10.00"
 :revision 4

 :typed "12."

 ;; The caller establishes a NEW baseline, different from the mount-time
 ;; one, at a new generation. The control must show what the caller
 ;; asserts NOW.
 :reset-baseline "77.77"
 :next-revision  5

 :displayed-after-reset "77.77"

 ;; Render purity. The read is a pure function of (record, generation,
 ;; baseline), so repeating it is free of consequence — which is what
 ;; makes a StrictMode double-invoke, a concurrent restart and an
 ;; abandoned candidate all uneventful rather than each needing a rule.
 ;; Three renders is enough to catch a first-render-only effect.
 :renders 3

 ;; Hot reload: the seam advances the cell's BODY generation. A buffered
 ;; draft lives in frame data with no host slot to index, so the record
 ;; and its stamp are untouched and there is no slot order to drift.
 :hot-reload-generation      7
 :record-after-hot-reload    {:reset-key 4 :draft "12."}
 :displayed-after-hot-reload "12."}
